findings.finding:
The R1-7 disposition says the model 'raises ValueError on bad shapes'. An unhashable entry in an item's or a disposition's decisions list (for example [['x']]) still raises TypeError from set(). Other gaps remain:
- a disposition's decisions may be empty or unrelated to its items' decisions (both accepted in scratch);
- unused obligations are accepted;
- obligation ids named in item text (O-SERVER-MEMFD in A4, O-W03-GROUPS in A10) are not checked against the obligations list;
- dates are not format-checked;
- baseLines is still not checked against the base;
- dropping a carried item's only CARRIED amendment and re-pointing its disposition at another item passes (the model cannot judge semantics).
The five cases the brief names are all refused with ValueError. So are the coordinated base change (base digest now a constant), a dropped or reordered disposition, a changed carriedToW01 list, and a later item rewriting an earlier one.

questionAnswers.Q3:
Yes, verified in scratch with ValueError:
- a changed base, including a coordinated base-plus-record change, now that the digest is a model constant;
- a missing current text, or a wrong count;
- an undecided owner decision;
- an item citing an unknown decision;
- a published effective file that differs;
- a dropped disposition;
- a changed carriedToW01 list;
- a later item that rewrites an earlier one.
Remaining gaps: an EDITORIAL item can change normative text without any decision (R2-3), and some malformed shapes raise TypeError (R2-4).
